-- title:
--   Twisted stabiliser modulo centre of a diagonal GL₂ element
-- statement:
--   Let $L/K$ be a finite Galois extension of fields (given as a finite-dimensional Galois $K$-algebra $L$), and let $\sigma$ be a $K$-algebra automorphism of $L$ such that every $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/K)$ lies in the subgroup of integer powers of $\sigma$, so that $\sigma$ generates the Galois group. Let $t \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ be diagonal, in the sense that its $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ entries vanish, and assume $\mathrm{N}_{L/K}(t_{00}/t_{11}) \neq 1$. Write $\sigma$ also for the entrywise action on $\mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ induced by $\sigma$ viewed as a ring homomorphism. Two assertions are made. First, there is a subgroup $\Lambda \le \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ whose members are exactly the $\gamma \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ with $t^{-1}\bigl(\gamma\, t\, \sigma(\gamma)^{-1}\bigr)$ in the centre of $\mathrm{GL}_2(L)$; that is, the set of such $\gamma$ is a subgroup. Second, for every $\gamma \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$, the condition $t^{-1}\bigl(\gamma\, t\, \sigma(\gamma)^{-1}\bigr) \in Z(\mathrm{GL}_2(L))$ holds if and only if either $\gamma$ is diagonal ($\gamma_{10} = \gamma_{01} = 0$) and $\gamma_{00}/\gamma_{11}$ lies in the image of $K$ in $L$, or $\gamma$ is antidiagonal ($\gamma_{00} = \gamma_{11} = 0$) and $\sigma(\gamma_{01}/\gamma_{10})\,(t_{00}/t_{11})^2 = \gamma_{01}/\gamma_{10}$.
--
--   This identifies, for a diagonal $t$ of non-trivial twisted norm class, the stabiliser of $t$ under $\sigma$-twisted conjugation modulo the centre: it is the group generated by the $K$-rational diagonal torus together with the centre, enlarged by an antidiagonal coset precisely when the displayed equation $\sigma(w)(t_{00}/t_{11})^2 = w$ is solvable. It is used in the analysis of twisted orbital integrals and $\sigma$-conjugacy classes for $\mathrm{GL}_2$, in particular by the lemmas describing twisted stabilisers as diagonal or antidiagonal and by the computations of twisted orbital contributions to trace identities.
-- source:
